              "reasoning": {
                "text": "The legal week in force on each decree date and at the end of the record; exact readings of the supplied path.\n\nD\u00e9cret du 27 octobre 1936 (metalworking and iron-steel), via Willemyns and McClements, Matignon counterfactual: technical appendix (2026): Art. 2: eight hours over five days, 6h40 over six, or unequal daily hours of at most eight; the forty-hour week for metalworking and iron and steel. [1]\n\nInternational Labour Office, I.L.O. Year-Book 1939-40 (1940), pp. 44-45: Provisional general 45-hour week from 21 April 1939; wartime decree of 1 September 1939 allowing up to 60 hours in all occupations. [2]",
                "reference_urls": [
                  "https://docs.google.com/document/d/17baf2aiCkJ-IzOSfVK0JnFegOkOoaCAolTBoC-guhk8/edit",
                  "https://webapps.ilo.org/public/libdoc/ilo/P/09614/09614%281939-1940%29"
                ]
              }
            }
          },
          "reasoning": {
            "text": "Legal weekly-hours regime for French industry, hours per week: 48 under the 1919 eight-hour law, 40 from the 27 October 1936 metalworking decree, 45 from the provisional 21 April 1939 week and 60 from the 1 September 1939 wartime decree. A recorded input the question intervenes on.",
            "reference_urls": []
          }
        },
